    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required rejecting owner creation with **409** when the target city already contains 50 or more owners. I implemented this following the existing OfficeFloor conflict-check pattern (modeled on the household-uniqueness check):\n\n1. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`** (function) \u2014 reads the validated `OwnerFieldsDto` via `@Val`, counts existing owners in the same city case-insensitively (matching `AssignCustomerCode`'s per-city counting convention), and throws `OwnerCityCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50.\n\n2. **`OwnerCityCapacityException`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause for the escalation machinery.\n\n3. **`OwnerCityCapacityExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT` with `{\"errors\": [\"city\"]}`, consistent with the other conflict handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `officefloor/escalation/\u2026OwnerCityCapacityException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **Wired the step** into `owners.POST.yml` as `checkCityCapacity`, inserted after `checkHouseholdUnique` and before `build`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors \u2014 so the count reflects owners already persisted in the transaction and the check happens before the new owner is built/saved.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fills a fresh city to exactly 50, expects the 51st to get 409): **passes**.\n- Full `mvnw test` suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
